// ————— Landmarks: the buildings of "Your tsumiki journey" —————
// HAND-MAINTAINED. Not generated by build-vite-app.py.
//
// The list is Session 39.2's (Notion, "A Landmark for Every Prefecture, and 182
// for the Map", Oct 13 2026): 182 active historic sites, every prefecture at
// least once. Numbers (`n`) are that journal's, so a site can be looked up
// there; the gaps are its reserve (47, 55, 56, 99, 100, 129, 130, 133, 138).
// Lloyd, Oct 15 2026: with this many landmarks the small-building village is
// cut — landmarks alone carry the "about one a week" rhythm (lib/blocks.js).
//
// Rules the list was chosen under, which any addition must keep:
//   · historic buildings only — nothing modern with a managed name or likeness
//   · readings are shown on their own line, never as half-size furigana
//   · NO BLURBS YET. The blurb source is undecided (Session 39), so a site
//     carries only its names. `note` is for whoever writes the blurb — it says
//     what the blurb must disclose (a reconstruction, a closure) — and is never
//     shown in the app.
//
// Two characters differ from the journal on purpose — both are one-codepoint
// slips of the kind CLAUDE.md warns about, settled by the place's real name:
//   #146 鞆の浦 (鞆 U+9786), the journal has 韞 (U+97DE)
//   #178 櫛田神社 (櫛 U+6ADB), the journal has 櫓 (U+6AD3)
//
// Map positions are real latitude/longitude, projected exactly as the map's
// outline was (see MAP_PROJECTION). Okinawa sits in an inset box in the empty
// sea at the top left, at its own scale.
//
// Every site is drawn by a builder for its family (keep, pagoda, gate, hall,
// bridge, …) with a few parameters, and a handful are one-offs. A building's
// grid is rows of slot letters, top row first; "." is empty. The letter says
// which of the learner's blocks it prefers (PREFER below) — never anything
// about the block's worth.

